# ⭐ 4. Defining Member Functions

There are **2 ways**:

---

# ⭐ A) **Definition OUTSIDE class**

### 📌 Steps:
1. Declare function inside class.
2. Define using **scope resolution (::)** outside class.

### Example

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class item {
    int number;
    float cost;
public:
    void getdata(int a, float b); // declaration
    void putdata();               // declaration
};

// definition OUTSIDE class
void item::getdata(int a, float b) {
    number = a;
    cost = b;
}

void item::putdata() {
    cout << "Number = " << number << endl;
    cout << "Cost = " << cost << endl;
}

int main() {
    item x;
    x.getdata(20, 50.5);
    x.putdata();
}
```

---

# ⭐ B) **Definition INSIDE class**

Inside = automatically **inline**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class item {
    int number;
    float cost;

public:
    void getdata(int a, float b) {   // defined inside
        number = a;
        cost = b;
    }

    void putdata() {                // inline function
        cout << number << endl;
        cout << cost << endl;
    }
};
```

---

# ⭐ Difference Summary (Easy Table)

| Feature | Inside Class | Outside Class |
|--------|--------------|----------------|
| Definition | Inside body | Outside using `::` |
| Inline? | Yes (automatically) | No (unless explicitly inline) |
| Use case | Small functions | Large functions |

