# ✅ **CODE THAT CLEARLY SHOWS INLINE VS NORMAL FUNCTIONS**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// 1) Normal function (NOT inline)
void normalFunc() {
    cout << "This is a NORMAL function.\n";
}

// 2) Inline function (inline keyword)
inline void inlineFunc() {
    cout << "This is an INLINE function.\n";
}

// 3) Function inside a class (auto-inline)
class Demo {
public:
    void insideClass() {      // inside class = automatically inline
        cout << "This is a function defined INSIDE a class (auto-inline).\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    cout << "--- Calling Normal Function ---\n";
    normalFunc();

    cout << "--- Calling Inline Function ---\n";
    inlineFunc();

    cout << "--- Calling Inside-Class Function ---\n";
    Demo d;
    d.insideClass();

    return 0;
}
```

---

# ⭐ **What this code proves**

### 1️⃣ Normal function
Compiler **jumps** to function and returns.

### 2️⃣ Inline function
Compiler **replaces call with code**, no jump.

### 3️⃣ Inside-class function  
Compiler treats it as **inline automatically**, even if you don’t write `inline`.

