# ⭐ TOPIC 3 — Memory Allocation for Objects

This one is very simple.

### ✔ Member FUNCTIONS  
Stored **once** for the entire class  
(shared by ALL objects)

### ✔ Member VARIABLES  
Each object gets **its own copy**

---

# Visual Breakdown

```
             CLASS
   -------------------------
   | member function 1     |   <-- only one copy
   | member function 2     |
   -------------------------

         OBJECT 1   OBJECT 2   OBJECT 3
         --------   --------   --------
 number |   10   | |   20   | |   30   |
 cost   | 99.95 | | 120.50 | | 17.40  |
         --------   --------   --------
```

Every object has **its own data**, but **same functions**.

---

# ⭐ TOPIC 4 — Constant Member Functions (`const`)

A constant function:

✔ cannot change any class data  
✔ is declared with `const` at the end  

---

# Example

```cpp
class Bank {
    int accno;
    double balance;

public:
    double get_balance() const {   // constant function
        return balance;            // OK
        //balance += 100;          // ❌ error
    }
};
```

Why use it?

→ Prevent accidental modification  
→ Useful in security-sensitive classes  

---

# ⭐ TOPIC 5 — Static Data Members  
(shared by all objects, NOT separate copies)

---

# Simple Understanding

- Normal variable → each object gets a copy  
- `static` variable → only **one copy for entire class**

### ✔ characteristics:

- Initialized to zero  
- Shared by all objects  
- Exists for entire program  

---

# Simple Code

```cpp
class Item {
    static int count;
    int number;

public:
    void getdata(int a) {
        number = a;
        count++;
    }

    void putcount() {
        cout << "count: " << count << "\n";
    }
};

int Item::count;  // static variable defined outside
```

---

### Object behavior:

```cpp
Item a, b, c;

a.getdata(100);  // count = 1
b.getdata(200);  // count = 2
c.getdata(300);  // count = 3
```

ALL objects increase the same `count`.

---

# ⭐ TOPIC 6 — Static Member Functions

Rules:

✔ Can only access static members  
✔ Called using class name (no object needed)

---

# Example

```cpp
class Counter {
    static int count;

public:
    static void showCount() {
        cout << count;
    }

    void inc() {
        count++;
    }
};

int Counter::count = 0;
```

Call:

```cpp
Counter::showCount();
```

---

# ⭐ Full Summary (Exam-Perfect)

```
Nesting of member functions:
    A member function can call another member function directly without using object name.

Private member functions:
    They cannot be called by objects; only other member functions can call them.

Memory allocation:
    Only member variables get separate memory for each object.
    Member functions are stored once for the entire class.

Constant member functions:
    Declared with const keyword; cannot modify class data.

Static data members:
    Shared among all objects; only one copy; must be defined outside class.

Static member functions:
    Can access only static data; called using class name.
```

